<SiteShell title="About me | Mike Abner" activePath="/about/">
	<section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="about-title">
		<h2 id="about-title">Helping technology-driven companies turn ideas into valuable software products</h2>
		<h3 id="about-subtitle">25+ years as an engineer and technology leader helping companies build products, scale teams, and solve complex problems efficiently.</h3>

		<div class="page-copy">
			<p>
				It's easy to assume that after 25 years in software engineering, and spending the last several years as a CTO and VP of Engineering, the part of my job I enjoy most is leading large organizations.
			</p>

			<p>
				The truth is, it is still the whiteboard.
			</p>

			<p>
				I love taking an ambiguous business idea and turning it into a practical software solution. Give me a business problem, a product opportunity, or a customer challenge, and I want to understand what we are really trying to accomplish, explore the possible solutions, debate the tradeoffs, and figure out the simplest approach that can create meaningful impact.
			</p>

			<p>
				At my core, I am still an engineer. I care about architecture, performance, scalability, and writing good software. But I have always believed that software is a tool for solving problems, not the end goal itself.
			</p>

			<p>
				One phrase I often say to my engineering team is "We are software engineers. We solve problems. Sometimes we solve problems with code." That idea has shaped how I approach technology leadership. The value of a software system is not found in the elegance of its codebase. The value comes from how effectively it helps deliver impact for the business and its customers.
			</p>

			<p>
				I believe the best technology leaders sit at the intersection of business strategy, product thinking, and engineering execution. I enjoy working with CEOs, executives, and product and engineering leaders to understand the problem behind the request. Often the best solution is not the most complex one. It is the one that solves the right problem, can be delivered quickly, and gives the organization the ability to learn and adapt.
			</p>

			<p>
				Pragmatism has been one of the core principles throughout my career. Every technical decision is a tradeoff. Every additional layer of complexity has a cost. I believe great engineers build systems that are understandable, maintainable, and flexible enough to evolve as the business changes. The goal is not to predict every possible future scenario. The goal is to build the right solution for today while creating a foundation for tomorrow.
			</p>

			<p>
				Over my career, I have had the opportunity to lead engineering organizations through many different phases including rapid growth, organizational change, shifting market conditions, and major technology transitions. Each stage required different approaches, but the underlying challenge was always the same...how do we align engineering effort with the problems that matter most?
			</p>

			<p>
				The work I find most rewarding is helping teams find that alignment. Giving talented engineers the context they need to make good decisions. Helping business leaders understand what technology can make possible. Creating an environment where teams can move quickly while still building systems that last.
			</p>

			<p>
				I do not believe successful engineering organizations are built by having the smartest person in the room make every decision. They are built by creating clarity around the problem, empowering great people, and removing the obstacles that prevent them from doing their best work.
			</p>

			<p>
				Ultimately, that is what has motivated me throughout my career: helping organizations turn ideas into products, products into customer value, and technology into a competitive advantage.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>
</SiteShell>
